/-!
# Reaching a boundary point of a finite-type image by a DVR

Source: Stacks Project, limits.tex, Tag 0CM2
(limits-lemma-reach-point-closure-Noetherian), restricted to points outside the image.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-noetherian-image-boundary-dvr)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A point in the closure of a finite-type image, outside the image itself, is the
closed-point image of a DVR whose fraction-field spectrum lifts to the source.

Source: Stacks Project, limits.tex, Tag 0CM2
(limits-lemma-reach-point-closure-Noetherian), restricted to boundary points.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-noetherian-image-boundary-dvr)

Proof:
1. Quasi-compactness and [lem-sc-picard-closure-specialization] give x in U with
   z = g(x) specializing to y. Since y is not in the image, z differs from y.
   Choose an affine Noetherian neighbourhood V = Spec B of y [pqf-def-locnoeth].
   The generalization z lies in V. Local finite type gives an affine neighbourhood
   W = Spec C of x inside the preimage of V, with finite-type ring map B → C
   [def-weil-finite-type-morphism].
2. Let p represent y, r represent x, and q be the inverse image of r in B.
   Then q is strictly contained in p. Put L = κ(r) and R = (B/q) localized at p/q.
   The map B → C → L has kernel q and is nonzero on B outside p, so it embeds R
   into L. Its maximal ideal is (p/q)R and its fraction field is κ(q)
   [def-weil-affine-spectrum, def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime, def-weil-residue-field].
3. Quotients and localizations of Noetherian rings are Noetherian: ideals are
   generated by images of generators of their inverse images or contractions.
   Thus R is a nonzero Noetherian local domain. An element of p outside q gives
   a nonzero element of its maximal ideal, so R is not a field
   [def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring, def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime].
4. The finite-type residue-field lemma shows that L is finitely generated over κ(q)
   [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-residue-field-extension-finitely-generated]. Apply
   [lem-sfb-local-domain-fg-field-dvr] to obtain a DVR A inside L containing R,
   with fraction field L and maximal ideal contracting to the maximal ideal of R.
5. Take K = L. The residue map C → L defines u through W, and B → B/q → R → A
   defines v through V. Both composites from Spec L to V come from the same map
   B → L, so functoriality of Spec gives the square. The maximal ideal of A
   contracts to p in B, so v sends its closed point to y
   [def-weil-affine-spectrum, def-weil-residue-field, def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime]. -/
theorem exists_dvr_of_mem_closure_range_not_mem_range
    {U Y : Scheme.{u}} (g : U ⟶ Y) [LocallyOfFiniteType g] [QuasiCompact g]
    [IsLocallyNoetherian Y] (y : Y) (hy : y ∈ closure (Set.range g))
    (hy_not : y ∉ Set.range g) :
    ∃ (A : Type u) (_ : CommRing A) (_ : IsDomain A) (_ : IsDiscreteValuationRing A)
      (K : Type u) (_ : Field K) (_ : Algebra A K) (_ : IsFractionRing A K)
      (a : Spec (CommRingCat.of K) ⟶ U) (v : Spec (CommRingCat.of A) ⟶ Y),
      a ≫ g = Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap A K)) ≫ v ∧
        v (IsLocalRing.closedPoint A) = y := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
